# systemd USER unit of BOTH host users, RS_USER and RUN_USER (the same file): setup/04-timer.sh installs it as
# ~/.config/systemd/user/runner-updater.service, and runner-updater.timer starts it every 10 minutes
# (linux-docker/README.md, Install step 11 and "The updater"). One pass of the runner-image updater. ExecStart is the
# link Install step 10 makes (%h: the user's home), so each pass runs this release's ~/build. With no WorkingDirectory=,
# systemd runs a user service in the user's home (systemd.exec), with the user manager's environment and no terminal:
# the updater needs no working folder, sets its own PATH and needs nothing else of them.
[Unit]
Description=runner-updater pass: one pass of the runner-image updater (linux-docker/README.md, "The updater")

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=%h/bin/runner-updater pass
# THE BOUND. A oneshot has no start timeout unless it names one. RUN_USER's pass builds a release's image (02-d1.sh
# build V, as its child). 02-d1.sh's own bounded waits: 60 s (GitHub's API), 900 s (the tarball), 30 s (docker) and
# 20 s (the registry); the build was measured on the host at 7 min 38 s from the start of its step 5 to the image
# (2026-10-22, with the pull of the base image; 27 s with the base cached), and its checks after the build at 66 s:
# about 26 min in all. 1 h is above that. At the bound systemd stops the unit: SIGTERM to EVERY process of its cgroup.
# 02-d1.sh exits 143 (its own trap removes its build context); the updater waits for it (a TERM trap while the build
# runs), prints its CUT line and exits 0: no STOP, and the next pass builds the release again. A TERM that comes
# before 02-d1.sh starts is a CUT too ("no build started"). Not unconditional: systemd sends SIGKILL to what is left at
# TimeoutStopSec (this unit names none: the manager's default, DefaultTimeoutStopSec), and a process it kills runs no
# trap. Then there is no CUT line, the build context may stay (under ~/.cache/liveapp-ci), the updater lock is free once
# every holder has exited, and the state is valid (it is written only by rename): no STOP, and the next pass builds
# again. The updater's TERM WINDOW (its header) is such a case when the 02-d1.sh it forked after the TERM runs until
# then; when that 02-d1.sh ends first, the updater prints a CUT and writes no STOP, whatever it exited with (but 0:
# built), as it recorded the TERM.
# THE RESIDUAL: a build that hangs each time is cut each time, an hour each, then built again by the next pass, with no
# STOP and no end. RUN_USER's journal shows one CUT line per try (journalctl --user -u runner-updater), and its updater
# lock is held most of the time (a person's `runner-updater build V`, `on` and `off` wait for it). What reaches the
# owner is the watchdog's STALE ask (PLAN D8: a release out for 7 days that no family runs). RS_USER's pass of this
# release takes seconds; a later release that switches in a pass (its window waits for runscaler's own stop, up to
# 4260 s) needs another bound.
TimeoutStartSec=1h
